<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1646">1646</a>–<a href="/years/1716">1716</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Leibniz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz was a German philosopher, mathematician and scholar. He developed the differential and integral calculus independently of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>, designed a calculating machine and worked on binary arithmetic. His philosophy of monads argued that this is the best of all possible worlds. From 1676 he served the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> as librarian, historian and adviser, and he helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-academy-of-sciences">Berlin Academy</a> of Sciences in 1700.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz.jpg" alt="Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Bernhard Francke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christoph_Bernhard_Francke_-_Bildnis_des_Philosophen_Leibniz_(ca._1695).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
